# Applied migrations are checksummed BY BYTE, so their line endings are part of
# the contract.
#
# Prisma records a SHA-256 of each migration file when it applies it, and warns
# that the migration "was modified after it was applied" when the bytes on disk
# no longer hash to the recorded value. Its offer to resolve that is to reset
# the development database.
#
# Without this rule, a checkout on Windows rewrites every `.sql` to CRLF and
# every one of those checksums changes — so a developer who has migrated on one
# machine and cloned on another is told their history was tampered with, and
# invited to drop their database over a line ending. Pinning LF makes the file
# the same bytes everywhere.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Everything the server executes or parses, pinned to LF.
#
# A Windows checkout would otherwise rewrite these to CRLF, and every one of
# them then fails in a way that does not name the cause:
#
#   *.sh          `/usr/bin/env bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory`
#   *.service     systemd reads the trailing \r as part of the value, so an
#                 ExecStart path silently points at a file that does not exist
#   *.conf        nginx and MariaDB include the \r in a directive argument
#
# The files are LF in the repository already. This is what stops a copy made on
# a Windows machine - a colleague zipping the checkout and scp-ing it - from
# arriving broken.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.cnf     text eol=lf
.nvmrc    text eol=lf
